kanfei (0.1.0~beta33) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Security: fixes a critical information-disclosure hole introduced
    by beta31's public-droplet mode.  An unauthenticated visitor on a
    public droplet could reach `GET /api/db-admin/export/backup` and
    receive a full SQLite dump of the running database — including
    the plaintext `public_mode_ingest_secret` from `station_config`
    that the masking in `GET /api/config` never sees.  Two curls
    yielded indefinite write access to the ingest endpoints
    (impersonating the operator's private-station relay); a
    concurrent handful of the same call also DoS'd the small droplet
    that ships with the reference deployment because the copy runs
    synchronously and holds a temp file until the response completes.
    A new strict admin-only dependency (no public-mode bypass) now
    gates every admin GET whose response streams data an operator
    was trusted with privately: the SQLite dump, per-table JSON
    exports, DB stats, backup list and download, log stream, and API
    key listing.  Everything else remains browsable so the read-only
    Settings UI keeps rendering.  Verified live on
    `https://kanfei.vsits.co`.  Operators running beta31 or beta32 as
    a public droplet should upgrade immediately.

  * Security: tightens the guest-readable payload of `GET /api/config`
    on public droplets to drop ops metadata (`*_last_error`,
    `backup_last_success`, backup schedule/directory) and third-party
    account identifiers (Discord guild/channel IDs, Telegram chat ID,
    Weather Underground station ID, CWOP callsign, METAR station).
    The read-only Settings UI does not render those fields; a real
    admin on a private station still sees the full view.  A per-key
    seeded regression test would fail if a future edit introduces a
    typo in the hidden-key list (the class of bug that shipped in
    round one of this fix, caught by review).

  * Operational note for droplet operators: with these fixes in place,
    an nginx / Caddy-level shim that was blocking `/api/db-admin*`,
    `/api/backup*`, `/api/logs*`, and `/api/auth/api-keys*` at the
    reverse proxy is no longer required.  The application refuses
    those routes directly for unauthenticated callers on a public
    droplet.  Leaving the reverse-proxy shim in place is harmless
    (defense in depth); removing it is safe.

 -- Chris Nighswonger <dev@veritassuperaitsolutions.com>  Sat, 15 Aug 2026 11:38:31 +0000

kanfei (0.1.0~beta32) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixes the private-station relay so pushed data actually populates
    the remote droplet's dashboard.  The initial private → public
    push flow was silently succeeding on both ends — the private
    station's sender got 200 OK for every reading and identity push,
    the droplet's ingest endpoints accepted every request — yet the
    droplet showed nulls.  The two sides had been unit-tested
    independently and disagreed on the JSON body shape: sender was
    wrapping payloads in an extra ``{"snapshot": ...}`` / ``{"config":
    ...}`` layer while the ingest endpoints expected a flat mirror of
    the sensor snapshot / identity dict.  Every field got dropped on
    decode and every push buffered an empty record.  The wire is now
    flat, and a round-trip test stitches the sender's HTTP body
    through the endpoint's parser and the daemon-side field filter
    so a future re-wrap regression fails a test rather than shipping.

  * Fixes three read-only-Settings gaps on the public droplet.  The
    outer authentication guard was still redirecting unauthenticated
    visitors to a login page that a droplet has no account to fill —
    the guard now honours the read-only public-mode flag and lets
    guests reach the read-only Settings pane directly.  The map view's
    OpenStreetMap "Roads" base layer was 403'ing on the droplet
    because OpenStreetMap's tile server refuses requests from public
    production deployments; the Roads layer is now hidden in public
    mode, and if the operator's saved default happened to be Roads
    the map now falls back to the Carto layer (same OpenStreetMap
    data, rendered by a policy-friendly CDN) so the map never starts
    blank.  Finally, the location-picker map embedded in Settings has
    been switched from OpenStreetMap's raw tile source to Carto for
    the same reason.

  * Verified end-to-end since 2026-08-14 on a private Vantage Vue
    (vsits-02) pushing to a public droplet at kanfei.vsits.co:
    dashboard, live tiles, history charts, forecast, and Settings all
    render for unauthenticated guests, and every write returns 403 at
    the middleware layer.

